<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Admin Dashboard shell (ticket 10) + publish pipeline (ticket 13).

    Script-first (ADR-0009): this app owns NO behaviour. Every button resolves a
    verb through VerbRegistry and hands it to ScriptService, which runs an
    existing .ps1. Nothing here reimplements what the scripts already do.

    Package versions are pinned EXACTLY, with no floating ranges. WPF-UI is a
    UI framework: a floating range would let `dotnet restore` pick a different
    minor and change rendering with no code change at all.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <AssemblyName>KomorebiDashboard</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>KomorebiDashboard</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationIcon />
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="WPF-UI" Version="4.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ===================================================================
    Publish pipeline (ticket 13)

    Goal: ONE self-contained EXE in releases/ that runs on a machine with
    no .NET 8 runtime installed. Driven entirely from the CLI:

        dotnet publish src/KomorebiDashboard -c Release

    Every flag below was verified by producing the artefact and measuring
    it, not by reading documentation. Three of them contradict what the
    WPF-UI / .NET docs suggest, and those corrections are recorded inline.

    WHY PublishTrimmed IS false, AND MUST STAY false
      WPF is NOT trim-safe. Trimming removes members the trimmer cannot see
      are used, and WPF reaches almost everything by reflection: XAML type
      converters, styles, template triggers, resource lookup, and
      DataTemplate-driven content. The result is not a smaller app — it is
      an app that starts and then throws
      MissingMethodException / NullReferenceException when a view is
      realised, which is the worst failure mode there is because it looks
      fine in a smoke test that never opens a window.

      If the 175 MB download ever becomes a real problem, the first lever is
      PublishReadyToRun=false (roughly -40 MB, slower cold start), NOT
      trimming. Do not trade correctness for size here.

    WHY PublishSingleFile WITH IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract
      SingleFile alone still leaves native libraries (the WPF rendering
      stack, DirectWrite, DWM shims) beside the EXE, because the host cannot
      load a native DLL from inside its own bundle. With
      IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract=true they are embedded and
      unpacked to a temp directory on first run. The result is a genuinely
      single-file artefact that can be copied on its own.

      The cost is a first-run extraction delay. That is a priority-1
      (startup speed) regression and it was accepted knowingly: without it
      the "single file" claim is false and a clean machine fails to launch.

      SELF-CONTAINED, so no runtime prerequisite
    SelfContained=true bundles the .NET 8 runtime into the EXE. This is what
    removes the "you must install .NET Desktop Runtime" failure on a clean
    machine. It is also what makes the artefact ~175 MB rather than ~2 MB.

    WHY PublishReadyToRun (priority 1: maximum speed)
      R2R precompiles IL to native code at publish time, so startup skips
      JIT for the framework and the app's own hot paths. Measured: the app
      reaches a live window without the crossgen delay being paid at run time.

      WHY THE SIZE IS ACCEPTED (ticket 13 requires this be stated)
      Self-contained + R2R + untrimmed lands at roughly 175-180 MB for one
      EXE. That is a deliberate trade: it is large, and it buys a
      dependency-free installer artefact with fast startup. For a tool
      distributed as a local download rather than through the Microsoft
      Store, that is the right trade. It is recorded here so the number is
      never mistaken for a regression.

    SatelliteResourceLanguages=en (above) keeps satellite DLLs out of the
    bundle; English-only is the shipped language (ADR-0015).
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>false</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>
    <DebugType>none</DebugType>
    <PublishDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\releases\</PublishDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
